# =============================================================================
# Build-Log Tree (repo-root logs/)
# =============================================================================
# WHAT IS THERE, AND WHO WRITES IT. 50 per-plugin directories, 739 build logs,
# 51 MB at the time this rule was written. The sole writer is
# scripts/build-and-install.sh:92 (`local log_dir="logs/$PLUGIN_NAME"`), which
# drops one timestamped log per build into a per-plugin subdirectory of this
# repo-root tree. Every file under it is generated output. Nothing there is ever
# authored by hand.
#
# THIS RULE CHANGES NOTHING TODAY — AND IS STILL WORTH HAVING. Every file
# currently under the tree is ALREADY ignored: the 739 build logs by the `*.log`
# rule earlier in this file, the one stray `logs/.DS_Store` by the `.DS_Store`
# rule (both confirmed with `git check-ignore -v`). What was NOT covered is the
# DIRECTORY ITSELF and any file carrying a different extension — a JSON status
# dump, a text report, a WAV capture dropped beside a build log by some future
# harness. This repository has a public origin, so that next artifact is one
# wide `git add` away from permanent published history. The rule closes the NEXT
# artifact, not the current one. Do not delete it as redundant on the strength
# of the current contents.
#
# WHY A WHOLE-DIRECTORY RULE IS CORRECT HERE. The Installer block below, the
# O-Bowed golden block and the GSD sentinel block all deliberately match an
# ARTIFACT rather than its containing directory, because those directories also
# hold hand-authored source (installer.iss, install-readme text, tracked
# fixtures). A directory-wide rule there would silently stop new source from
# being tracked. This directory holds no source of any kind — it is generated
# output end to end — so here the directory IS the artifact.
#
# WHY THE LEADING SLASH. The anchor pins the rule to the repo root, the only
# place this tree exists (`find . -maxdepth 4 -type d -name logs` returns `./logs`
# alone). An UNANCHORED `logs/` would additionally swallow any future nested
# logs directory anywhere in the tree — a per-plugin or per-test one that might
# legitimately hold something tracked — and that failure is invisible: the file
# simply never appears in `git status`. The anchor is what keeps a correct
# directory rule from broadening into the exact failure mode the rest of this
# file exists to avoid.
#
# NOTHING WAS UNTRACKED BY THIS RULE. `git ls-files logs` was empty when it was
# authored, so no `git rm --cached` was needed — and none was run.
/logs/

# =============================================================================
# Installer / Distribution Packages
# =============================================================================
# Built installer packages are release artifacts, not source. They belong on
# GitHub Releases — which .github/workflows/build-and-release.yml already
# publishes to — and not in git, where every rebuild would store another
# multi-megabyte blob in history forever.
#
# `*.exe` and `*.zip` already appear elsewhere in this file and cover the
# Windows-installer and archive forms. The three rules below close the macOS
# installer / disk-image and Windows MSI gap rather than duplicating those.
#
# A wildcard rule over every plugin's `dist` folder was considered and
# DELIBERATELY REJECTED. Those folders also hold hand-authored source:
# O-Tremolo's Inno Setup script (`installer.iss`) and the install-readme text
# files for both O-Tremolo and O-Polystutter live in exactly such a folder. A
# directory-wide rule would silently stop new installer scripts from being
# tracked. Match on the built-artifact extension instead — do not broaden this
# to the containing directory.
*.pkg
*.dmg
*.msi

# Per-session working files (probes, gate output, build logs). Never a deliverable.
scratchpad/

# =============================================================================
# GSD Run-Scoped State
# =============================================================================
# The agent-dispatch isolation sentinel is run-scoped state, not source:
# `gsd-tools query dispatch-isolation` rewrites its `written_at` timestamp as an
# unconditional side effect of resolving isolation, so every session left it
# dirty in `git status` — noise in a repo whose commit discipline depends on
# reading `git status --short` accurately immediately before every commit.
#
# A directory-wide `.gsd/` rule was considered and DELIBERATELY REJECTED, for
# the same reason as the plugin `dist` wildcard above: it would silently stop a
# future file placed there — one that SHOULD be tracked — from ever being seen.
# Ignore the single path. If GSD later writes more run-scoped siblings there,
# widening this is a one-line follow-up made on purpose.
.gsd/dispatch-isolation-sentinel.json
